#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Forced command for the Logwatch board (role l4waf of logwatch-remote) on a port proxy: /usr/local/sbin/logwatch-nfwafctl.

The proxy forwards raw TCP/UDP traffic (DNAT: app servers, SFTP, anything not HTTP). This is the L2-L4 layer, plus fixed
payload bytes (L7 signatures of non-HTTP protocols, e.g. UDP query floods).
SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND: "apply" (desired state as JSON on stdin) | "confirm <gen>" | "status" | "hits [hours]" | "rollback"
| "check" | "render" | "list" | "clear <list> [ip]". The board never sends nft text: every field is checked here and the
ruleset is rendered locally.

Managed state (nothing else is touched): the nft tables "inet lw_waf" and "netdev lw_waf_in" and their file
/etc/logwatch-waf/lw-waf.nft (loaded at boot by logwatch-nft.service). Two stages:
  early   netdev ingress on the public interface, before conntrack and defragmentation: stateless packet checks
          (fragments, bad TCP flags, tiny MSS, TTL, size, bogon sources, MAC/VLAN). Early rules must name ports, and a port
          this host itself listens on (sshd, WireGuard, ...) is refused, so management can't be cut.
  conn    forward hook, only traffic that was port-forwarded (ct status dnat) and arrived on the public interface: bans,
          sources (IPs / countries / networks), connection state, rate / bandwidth / concurrent-connection limits per IP or
          per /24 (/64), fixed payload bytes, reverse-path (spoofed source) check, allow-list exceptions, OS fingerprint
          (osf, needs the pf.os database loaded), "add the source to a dynamic list" (auto-ban).
  pre     prerouting, after conntrack and before DNAT, on the public interface: invalid conntrack state. Like early rules
          these must name ports nobody on this host listens on.
Dynamic lists (v3) are timeout sets filled by rules and by the port-scan trap (new connections to closed ports: neither
forwarded nor used by this host); an enforced list drops its members on forwarded traffic only, never on this host's own
ports. Their members survive an apply and a rollback.
Each apply checks the file with `nft -c`, loads it atomically, keeps the previous generations for rollback, and arms a
dead-man timer: unless the board confirms over a fresh SSH connection within CONFIRM_SECS, the previous ruleset comes back.
Payload v1 ({"l4": [rate|geo rules]}, boards before 2.8.0) is still accepted.
"""
import fcntl
import ipaddress
import json
import os
